   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to parking in\nelectric vehicle charging spaces",
    "basePrintNo": "S6836",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6836-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 5,
       "fullName": "Jim Gaughran",
       "imgName": "1042_Jim_Gaughran.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1222,
       "sessionMemberId": 1220,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "GAUGHRAN"
      },
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 11,
       "fullName": "John Liu",
       "imgName": "1045_John_Liu.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1225,
       "sessionMemberId": 1223,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "LIU"
      }
     ],
     "size": 2
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1202, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6836               Revised 7/21/2020\n\nSPONSOR: METZGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to parking in\nelectric vehicle charging spaces\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL::\n\nA bill to amend the vehicle and traffic law by prohibiting parking in\nspots designated for charging electric vehicles.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS::\n\nSection 1- Amends section 1202 of the vehicle and traffic law relating\nto \"Stopping, standing or parking in specified places\". This bill would\nmake it a violation of the Vehicle and Traffic Law for a non-electric\nvehicle to park in a space designated for electric charging. Electric\nvehicles must also be charging in order to utilize the spot.\n\nSection 2 states the effective date - 180th day.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS::\n\nCurrently, it is a violation of this section to stop, stand or park a\nvehicle, on the roadway side of a stopped vehicle, on a sidewalk, in an\nintersection, obstructing traffic, railroad tracks, and on a divided\nhighway.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION::\n\nIn recent years, the number of electric vehicles in New York has steadi-\nly risen. In 2018, NYSERDA estimated that there are 36,854 vehicles,\nwhich represents a 63% increase form 24,551 vehicles in 2017. NYSERDA\nhas set a goal of 850,000 vehicles by 2025 and two million vehicles by\n2030. The resulting benefit of reaching this goal would be $5.1 billion\nin societal benefits. The transportation sector is responsible for\napproximately a third of all greenhouse gas emissions in the nation. In\norder to meet the state's ambitious climate goals, efforts must be\nundertaken to reduce emissions from the transportation sector including\nlimiting emissions from fossil fuel powered vehicles by converting to\nzero-emission or low-emission electric vehicles. In order for consumers\nto make that leap, charging stations must be made available.\n\nTypically, electric vehicles are charged at home. However, there are\npublic charging stations available for charging vehicles. Public elec-\ntric vehicle charging stations are limited in number and are often found\nin parking lots, where other vehicles may be parked.  According to the\nAlternative Fuels Data Center, there are nearly 1,500 stations for elec-\ntric vehicles in New York State, which offer a total of 3,614 charging\noutlets. As electric vehicles continue to be adopted by consumers, it is\ncritical that parking spaces designated for charging are available and\nthat such designation is enforceable.  Several states have passed \"anti-\nicing\" provisions. \"Anti-icing\" laws make it a violation for non-elec-\ntric vehicles to park in spaces designated for charging. Currently in\nNew York, there is no such provision. This legislation will make it a\nviolation of the vehicle and traffic law for non-electric vehicles to\npark in spaces designated for charging.\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY::\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS::\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE::\n\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become a law.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
